<p>A few quick questions. Sorry if I am missing something obvious or some b=
ackground.<u></u><u></u></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">On 04.12.24 08:04, Valery Smyslov wrote: <u></u><u><=
/u></p>
<blockquote style=3D"margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt">
<pre>note, that UTA WG has issued a WGLC for draft-ietf-uta-require-tls13-0=
2 (New Protocols Must Require TLS 1.3) [1].<u></u><u></u></pre>
<pre><u></u>=C2=A0<u></u></pre>
<pre> [1] <a href=3D"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-uta-requir=
e-tls13/" target=3D"_blank">https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-uta=
-require-tls13/</a><u></u><u></u></pre>
</blockquote>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">Thanks for pointer to this. It looks like a more det=
ailed version of tls12-frozen draft. Is there a good reason not to merge th=
e two documents? Is it due to different WGs? or different intended status? =
or something else?
<u></u><u></u></p>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><u></u>=C2=A0<u></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><u></u>=C2=A0<u></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">On 04.12.24 10:36, John Mattsson wrote:<u></u><u></u=
></p>
</div>
<blockquote style=3D"margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt">
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><span style=3D"font-size:11pt">as-is, TLS 1.3 does n=
ot provide excellent security for long-term connections.</span><u></u><u></=
u></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Do we have an I-D which defines <i>how long</i> do we consider as long-t=
erm connections? or I-D which gives recommendations or best practices for
<i>how long </i>do we consider TLS 1.3 to provide excellent security?<u></u=
><u></u></p>
<p>---<u></u><u></u></p>
<p>Considering the following two statements in I-D, I have two questions: <=
u></u><u></u></p>
<pre style=3D"background:white;box-sizing:border-box;white-space:pre-wrap;f=
ont-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;text-align:start;text=
-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;overflow:auto;word-=
spacing:0px"><span style=3D"font-size:10.5pt;color:rgb(33,37,41)">&gt;=C2=
=A0=C2=A0 For TLS it is important to note that the focus of these efforts i=
s<u></u><u></u></span></pre>
<pre style=3D"background:white"><span style=3D"font-size:10.5pt;color:rgb(3=
3,37,41)">&gt;=C2=A0=C2=A0 TLS 1.3 or later.=C2=A0 Put bluntly, post-quantu=
m cryptography for TLS 1.2<u></u><u></u></span></pre>
<pre style=3D"background:white"><span style=3D"font-size:10.5pt;color:rgb(3=
3,37,41)">&gt;=C2=A0=C2=A0 WILL NOT be supported.<u></u><u></u></span></pre=
>
<p>To me the two sentences are contradicting. Which one of the following is=
 intended?
<u></u><u></u></p>
<ol start=3D"1" type=3D"1">
<li class=3D"MsoNormal">
(My understanding from 1st sentence) Some PQC support for TLS 1.2 will stil=
l continue but it will not be the focus.=C2=A0
<u></u><u></u></li><li class=3D"MsoNormal">
(My understanding from 2nd sentence) We will exclusively work on PQC for TL=
S 1.3 or later.
<u></u><u></u></li></ol>
<p>What does the capitalization of WILL NOT mean? I did not find any such c=
apitalization in RFC 2119 and RFC 8174. Please add the relevant RFC in sect=
ion 2 or define it.
<u></u><u></u></p>
<pre style=3D"background:white;box-sizing:border-box;white-space:pre-wrap;f=
ont-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;text-align:start;text=
-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;overflow:auto;word-=
spacing:0px"><span style=3D"font-size:10.5pt;color:rgb(33,37,41)">&gt;=C2=
=A0=C2=A0 This<u></u><u></u></span></pre>
<pre style=3D"background:white"><span style=3D"font-size:10.5pt;color:rgb(3=
3,37,41)">&gt;=C2=A0=C2=A0 document specifies that outside of urgent securi=
ty fixes, no new<u></u><u></u></span></pre>
<pre style=3D"background:white"><span style=3D"font-size:10.5pt;color:rgb(3=
3,37,41)">&gt;=C2=A0=C2=A0 features will be approved for TLS 1.2.<u></u><u>=
</u></span></pre>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">If the intention of draft was #2 above, cross-readin=
g with this sentence, are we implying that PQC is not an urgent security is=
sue?
<u></u><u></u></p>
